emained essentially unchanged during 2004-2010.\n","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":512,"renderedHeight":276,"naturalWidth":512,"naturalHeight":276,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 526 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":7688},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m6005a2f2.gif","alt":"The figure above shows the percentage of current light, moderate, and heavy smokers, by year, based on number of cigarettes smoked per day in Minnesota during 1999-2010. The daily average number of cigarettes smoked by current smokers decreased from 14.3 in 1999 to 12.2 in 2010. In addition, the proportion of current smokers who smoked ≥25 cigarettes per day decreased steadily, from 14.3% in 1999 to 6.3% in 2010. From 2007 to 2010, the proportion of current smokers who smoked ≤15 cigarettes per day increased from 54.1% to 63.2%.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":524,"renderedHeight":363,"naturalWidth":524,"naturalHeight":363,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 534 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":12154},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m6005a2f3.gif","alt":"The figure shows packs of cigarettes sold per capita each year in Minnesota and the United States during 1999-2009. The decrease in both smoking prevalence and cigarettes smoked per day corresponds with a decrease in sales of cigarettes. Per capita cigarette pack sales in Minnesota decreased 40% from 1999 to 2009.s","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":522,"renderedHeight":336,"naturalWidth":522,"naturalHeight":336,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 316 characters — consi